% Tracker Protocol: HTTP Tracker Communication
% Chapter 3 of the literate BitTorrent client

\section{Tracker Protocol}

A tracker is a server that helps peers find each other. When our client wants
to download a torrent, it contacts the tracker to announce its presence and
receive a list of other peers sharing the same content. This chapter implements
HTTP tracker communication as specified in BEP 3.

\subsection{Tracker Error Types}

Communicating with a tracker can fail in several ways. We define a dedicated
error type that distinguishes between different failure modes, enabling
appropriate recovery strategies:

\begin{description}
\item[HTTP errors] Network-level failures: connection refused, DNS resolution
  failure, TLS handshake errors, or HTTP status codes indicating server errors.
  These are typically transient and worth retrying with backoff.

\item[Tracker error response] The tracker successfully responded but indicated
  an error via the ``failure reason'' field in its bencoded response. This
  happens when the tracker rejects our request---perhaps the torrent is not
  registered, or the info hash is banned. These are usually not worth retrying.

\item[Invalid response] The tracker returned data that isn't valid bencode,
  is missing required fields, or has fields of unexpected types. This suggests
  a misbehaving or incompatible tracker.

\item[Timeout] The tracker didn't respond within the allotted time (30 seconds
  per the PRD). This is transient and worth retrying, possibly with a different
  tracker if announce-list provides alternatives.
\end{description}

<<tracker errors>>=
/// Errors that can occur when communicating with a BitTorrent tracker.
///
/// These errors are categorized by their likely cause and appropriate recovery
/// strategy. HTTP errors and timeouts are typically transient and worth retrying.
/// Error responses from the tracker usually indicate a permanent problem with
/// the request. Invalid responses suggest tracker incompatibility.
#[derive(Debug, thiserror::Error)]
pub enum TrackerError {
    /// HTTP-level failure during tracker communication.
    ///
    /// This wraps errors from the HTTP client library (reqwest), including:
    /// - Connection refused or reset
    /// - DNS resolution failure
    /// - TLS/SSL handshake errors
    /// - HTTP 4xx/5xx status codes
    ///
    /// These errors are often transient. Retry with exponential backoff is
    /// recommended before giving up.
    #[error("HTTP request failed: {0}")]
    TrackerHttpError(#[from] reqwest::Error),

    /// The tracker returned a valid response containing a failure reason.
    ///
    /// Per BEP 3, trackers indicate errors by returning a bencoded dictionary
    /// with a "failure reason" key containing a human-readable error message.
    /// Common reasons include:
    /// - "torrent not registered with this tracker"
    /// - "invalid info_hash"
    /// - "client banned"
    ///
    /// These errors are usually permanent for the given request. Retrying
    /// with the same parameters is unlikely to succeed.
    #[error("tracker returned error: {0}")]
    TrackerErrorResponse(String),

    /// The tracker's response could not be parsed or was missing required fields.
    ///
    /// This error occurs when:
    /// - The response body is not valid bencode
    /// - Required fields (like "peers") are missing
    /// - Fields have unexpected types (e.g., "interval" is a string instead of integer)
    ///
    /// The contained string describes what was wrong with the response.
    /// This suggests tracker incompatibility or a bug in the tracker software.
    #[error("invalid tracker response: {0}")]
    TrackerInvalidResponse(String),

    /// The tracker did not respond within the timeout period.
    ///
    /// The timeout is 30 seconds as specified in the PRD. This error is
    /// distinct from HTTP timeout errors (which are wrapped in TrackerHttpError)
    /// to allow for explicit handling of announce timeouts.
    ///
    /// Timeouts are transient. The client should retry, possibly with a
    /// different tracker from the announce-list if available.
    #[error("tracker request timed out after 30 seconds")]
    TrackerTimeout,
}
@

The error enum uses [[thiserror]]'s derive macro to automatically implement
the [[std::error::Error]] trait. The [[#[from]]] attribute on [[TrackerHttpError]]
enables automatic conversion from [[reqwest::Error]] using the [[?]] operator,
which simplifies error handling in async code.

Note that [[TrackerHttpError]] wraps [[reqwest::Error]] directly rather than
converting it to a string. This preserves the original error's details (like
HTTP status codes) for debugging while still providing a clean display message.

