\subsection{Tracker Request Parameters}

Before we can announce to a tracker, we need to gather all the parameters that
the BitTorrent protocol requires. BEP 3 specifies these parameters precisely,
and getting them right is essential for successful tracker communication.

The parameters fall into three categories:

\begin{description}
\item[Identity parameters] The [[info_hash]] identifies which torrent we're
  interested in, while [[peer_id]] identifies our specific client instance.
  Both are 20-byte binary values.

\item[Network parameters] The [[port]] tells the tracker what port we're
  listening on, so other peers can connect to us.

\item[Progress parameters] The [[uploaded]], [[downloaded]], and [[left]]
  fields report our current transfer statistics. Trackers use these for
  statistics and may use them to prioritize peer selection.

\item[Control parameters] The [[event]] field signals lifecycle events
  (started, stopped, completed), while [[compact]] requests the efficient
  BEP-23 peer list format.
\end{description}

\subsubsection{Tracker Events}

The [[event]] parameter is optional in most announces, but \emph{must} be
sent for certain lifecycle events. BEP 3 defines three events:

\begin{description}
\item[started] Sent on the first announce when a download begins. The tracker
  uses this to add us to the swarm and may return a larger peer list for
  initial seeding.

\item[stopped] Sent when the client shuts down gracefully. This allows the
  tracker to immediately remove us from the swarm, rather than waiting for
  our entry to expire. Clients that crash or lose network connectivity
  cannot send this event, so trackers must handle missing ``stopped'' events.

\item[completed] Sent when the download finishes (all pieces verified). This
  only happens once per torrent---if we start the client with an already-complete
  torrent, we don't send ``completed'' again. Trackers use this to update their
  statistics on seeders vs. leechers.
\end{description}

Regular periodic announces (after the first ``started'') omit the event
parameter entirely. This is represented as [[None]] in our enum wrapper.

<<tracker event enum>>=
/// Events that can be sent to the tracker during the torrent lifecycle.
///
/// BEP 3 defines three events that signal state changes to the tracker.
/// Regular periodic announces omit the event parameter entirely.
///
/// # Protocol Details
///
/// - `started`: First announce, adds us to the swarm
/// - `stopped`: Graceful shutdown, removes us from swarm immediately
/// - `completed`: Download finished, transition from leecher to seeder
///
/// Note: If we start with an already-complete torrent (resuming as seeder),
/// we should send `started`, not `completed`.
#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub enum TrackerEvent {
    /// First announce when starting a download.
    ///
    /// Must be sent on the very first announce for a torrent. The tracker
    /// adds us to the swarm and typically returns a larger initial peer list.
    Started,

    /// Graceful shutdown notification.
    ///
    /// Should be sent when the client is shutting down cleanly. This allows
    /// the tracker to remove us from the swarm immediately rather than
    /// waiting for our entry to expire (typically 30-60 minutes).
    Stopped,

    /// Download completed notification.
    ///
    /// Sent exactly once when all pieces have been downloaded and verified.
    /// After this, we are a seeder (though this client doesn't implement
    /// seeding). Do not send if starting with an already-complete torrent.
    Completed,
}

impl TrackerEvent {
    /// Returns the event string as it appears in the tracker URL.
    ///
    /// BEP 3 specifies these exact strings: "started", "stopped", "completed".
    pub fn as_str(&self) -> &'static str {
        match self {
            TrackerEvent::Started => "started",
            TrackerEvent::Stopped => "stopped",
            TrackerEvent::Completed => "completed",
        }
    }
}
@

\subsubsection{The Request Structure}

Now we define a struct that holds all parameters needed for a tracker announce.
This struct serves as both documentation (what does an announce need?) and as
a clean interface for the announce function we'll implement later.

<<tracker request struct>>=
/// All parameters required for a tracker announce request.
///
/// This struct captures the complete state needed to construct an announce URL.
/// It is constructed by the client before each announce and used to build the
/// HTTP GET request to the tracker.
///
/// # BEP 3 Parameters
///
/// | Parameter | Type | Description |
/// |-----------|------|-------------|
/// | `info_hash` | 20 bytes | SHA1 hash of the bencoded info dictionary |
/// | `peer_id` | 20 bytes | Client's unique identifier for this session |
/// | `port` | integer | Port number client is listening on |
/// | `uploaded` | integer | Total bytes uploaded since `started` event |
/// | `downloaded` | integer | Total bytes downloaded since `started` event |
/// | `left` | integer | Number of bytes still needed (0 = seeding) |
/// | `event` | string | Optional: started, stopped, or completed |
/// | `compact` | flag | Request compact peer list (BEP-23) |
///
/// # Example
///
/// ```ignore
/// let request = TrackerRequest {
///     info_hash: torrent.info_hash,
///     peer_id: generate_peer_id(),
///     port: 6881,
///     uploaded: 0,
///     downloaded: 0,
///     left: torrent.total_length,
///     event: Some(TrackerEvent::Started),
///     compact: true,
/// };
/// ```
#[derive(Debug, Clone)]
pub struct TrackerRequest {
    /// SHA1 hash of the bencoded info dictionary.
    ///
    /// This 20-byte value uniquely identifies the torrent. It must match
    /// exactly what the tracker expects, computed from the original .torrent
    /// file's info dictionary.
    pub info_hash: [u8; 20],

    /// Unique identifier for this client session.
    ///
    /// This 20-byte value is generated once at startup (see [`generate_peer_id`])
    /// and reused for all announces and peer handshakes. It follows the
    /// Azureus-style format defined in BEP 20.
    pub peer_id: [u8; 20],

    /// Port number the client is listening on for incoming peer connections.
    ///
    /// Standard BitTorrent ports are 6881-6889, but any valid port works.
    /// If we're behind NAT without port forwarding, peers won't be able to
    /// connect to us, but we can still connect to them.
    pub port: u16,

    /// Total number of bytes uploaded since the "started" event.
    ///
    /// This is cumulative across all peers for this torrent session.
    /// Trackers use this for statistics and may factor it into peer selection.
    /// Set to 0 for the initial announce.
    pub uploaded: u64,

    /// Total number of bytes downloaded since the "started" event.
    ///
    /// This counts raw bytes received, including bytes from pieces that
    /// later failed hash verification. Set to 0 for the initial announce.
    pub downloaded: u64,

    /// Number of bytes still needed to complete the download.
    ///
    /// Calculated as `total_torrent_size - verified_bytes`. When this reaches
    /// 0, we have all pieces and can send the "completed" event.
    pub left: u64,

    /// Optional event indicating a state change.
    ///
    /// - `Some(Started)`: First announce for this torrent
    /// - `Some(Stopped)`: Client is shutting down
    /// - `Some(Completed)`: Download just finished
    /// - `None`: Regular periodic announce
    pub event: Option<TrackerEvent>,

    /// Request compact peer list format (BEP-23).
    ///
    /// When true, the tracker should return peers as a byte string where
    /// each peer is 6 bytes: 4 bytes for IPv4 address + 2 bytes for port
    /// (all big-endian). This is more efficient than the dictionary format.
    ///
    /// Most modern trackers only support compact format, so this should
    /// typically be `true`.
    pub compact: bool,
}
@

The struct uses fixed-size arrays for [[info_hash]] and [[peer_id]] rather than
[[Vec<u8>]] because these are always exactly 20 bytes. This provides compile-time
guarantees about the size and avoids heap allocation.

The numeric types are chosen to match the protocol requirements:
\begin{itemize}
\item [[port]] is [[u16]] because TCP ports range from 0 to 65535.
\item [[uploaded]], [[downloaded]], and [[left]] are [[u64]] because torrents
  can be many terabytes in size, and cumulative upload statistics can exceed
  the torrent size.
\end{itemize}

