\subsection{Announce URL Construction}

With all the request parameters defined and URL encoding implemented, we can now
construct the complete announce URL. BEP 3 specifies that the client sends an
HTTP GET request to the tracker's announce URL with all parameters encoded in
the query string.

The URL construction process involves several steps:

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Determine the query separator}: If the announce URL already contains
  a question mark (indicating existing query parameters), we append with \texttt{\&}.
  Otherwise, we start the query string with \texttt{?}. Some trackers embed
  authentication tokens or other parameters in their announce URLs, so we must
  handle this case correctly.

\item \textbf{Encode binary parameters}: The [[info_hash]] and [[peer_id]] are
  20-byte binary values that must be percent-encoded. We use our [[url_encode]]
  function from the previous section.

\item \textbf{Format numeric parameters}: The [[port]], [[uploaded]], [[downloaded]],
  and [[left]] values are formatted as decimal strings. No encoding is needed
  since digits are unreserved characters.

\item \textbf{Include optional parameters}: The [[event]] parameter is only
  included when present (started, stopped, or completed). Regular periodic
  announces omit this parameter entirely.

\item \textbf{Request compact format}: When [[compact]] is true, we include
  \texttt{compact=1}. This requests the more efficient BEP-23 peer list format
  where each peer is represented as 6 bytes instead of a dictionary.
\end{enumerate}

The order of query parameters does not matter per the HTTP specification, but
we use a consistent order that matches common client conventions. This makes
debugging easier when comparing URLs across different clients or examining
tracker logs.

<<announce url construction>>=
/// Construct the complete tracker announce URL from the base URL and request parameters.
///
/// This function builds the HTTP GET URL that will be sent to the tracker. It properly
/// handles URL encoding of binary parameters and correctly appends to URLs that may
/// already contain query parameters.
///
/// # Arguments
///
/// * `announce_url` - The base announce URL from the torrent's metainfo file
/// * `request` - The tracker request parameters
///
/// # Returns
///
/// A complete URL string ready to be used for an HTTP GET request.
///
/// # URL Structure
///
/// The resulting URL has this structure:
/// ```text
/// {announce_url}?info_hash={encoded}&peer_id={encoded}&port={port}
///   &uploaded={uploaded}&downloaded={downloaded}&left={left}
///   [&event={event}][&compact=1]
/// ```
///
/// # Example
///
/// ```ignore
/// let url = build_announce_url("http://tracker.example.com/announce", &request);
/// // Returns: "http://tracker.example.com/announce?info_hash=...&peer_id=...&..."
/// ```
pub fn build_announce_url(announce_url: &str, request: &TrackerRequest) -> String {
    // Determine if we need ? or & to start/continue the query string
    let separator = if announce_url.contains('?') { '&' } else { '?' };

    // Start building the URL
    let mut url = String::with_capacity(announce_url.len() + 256);
    url.push_str(announce_url);
    url.push(separator);

    // Required parameters (BEP 3)
    url.push_str("info_hash=");
    url.push_str(&url_encode(&request.info_hash));

    url.push_str("&peer_id=");
    url.push_str(&url_encode(&request.peer_id));

    url.push_str("&port=");
    url.push_str(&request.port.to_string());

    url.push_str("&uploaded=");
    url.push_str(&request.uploaded.to_string());

    url.push_str("&downloaded=");
    url.push_str(&request.downloaded.to_string());

    url.push_str("&left=");
    url.push_str(&request.left.to_string());

    // Optional event parameter
    if let Some(event) = &request.event {
        url.push_str("&event=");
        url.push_str(event.as_str());
    }

    // Compact peer list request (BEP 23)
    if request.compact {
        url.push_str("&compact=1");
    }

    url
}
@

The implementation uses [[String::with_capacity]] to pre-allocate enough space
for the typical announce URL. The capacity estimate of 256 bytes accounts for:
\begin{itemize}
\item Info hash: up to 60 characters (20 bytes, worst case all encoded)
\item Peer ID: up to 60 characters (same reasoning)
\item Numeric parameters: approximately 80 characters for typical values
\item Parameter names and separators: approximately 50 characters
\item Small buffer for the event parameter and compact flag
\end{itemize}

This avoids multiple reallocations during string building, which matters when
the client is announcing to many trackers rapidly.

\subsection{Announce URL Tests}

We test the URL construction with various configurations to ensure correctness.

<<announce url tests>>=
#[test]
fn build_announce_url_basic() {
    let request = TrackerRequest {
        info_hash: [0u8; 20],
        peer_id: [0u8; 20],
        port: 6881,
        uploaded: 0,
        downloaded: 0,
        left: 1000,
        event: Some(TrackerEvent::Started),
        compact: true,
    };

    let url = build_announce_url("http://tracker.example.com/announce", &request);

    assert!(url.starts_with("http://tracker.example.com/announce?"));
    assert!(url.contains("info_hash="));
    assert!(url.contains("peer_id="));
    assert!(url.contains("port=6881"));
    assert!(url.contains("uploaded=0"));
    assert!(url.contains("downloaded=0"));
    assert!(url.contains("left=1000"));
    assert!(url.contains("event=started"));
    assert!(url.contains("compact=1"));
}

#[test]
fn build_announce_url_with_existing_query_params() {
    // Some trackers include passkeys or other params in the announce URL
    let request = TrackerRequest {
        info_hash: [0xAAu8; 20],
        peer_id: [0xBBu8; 20],
        port: 51413,
        uploaded: 1024,
        downloaded: 2048,
        left: 0,
        event: None,
        compact: true,
    };

    let url = build_announce_url(
        "http://tracker.example.com/announce?passkey=secret123",
        &request,
    );

    // Should use & not ? since URL already has query params
    assert!(url.starts_with("http://tracker.example.com/announce?passkey=secret123&"));
    assert!(url.contains("&info_hash="));
    // Should not have two ?
    assert_eq!(url.matches('?').count(), 1);
}

